WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.461
The book runs one loop on every platform:
test with the native tool, detect drift,

2
00:00:05.461 --> 00:00:10.788
raise a typed task against the asset, fix
it natively, and close on the next test.

3
00:00:10.788 --> 00:00:12.349
Two rules matter most.

4
00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:16.536
The ticketing platform reads
control state but never writes it,

5
00:00:16.536 --> 00:00:19.063
and a task closes on a
re-test, not a click.

6
00:00:19.063 --> 00:00:22.819
On Microsoft 365, CISA's
ScubaGear is the main test,

7
00:00:22.819 --> 00:00:28.275
and its stable policy IDs let the mapping
from failure to task live as data.

8
00:00:28.275 --> 00:00:31.571
Conditional Access exceptions
get the same treatment,

9
00:00:31.571 --> 00:00:33.363
with an approver and an expiry.

10
00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:35.228
Compliance Manager is useful,

11
00:00:35.228 --> 00:00:38.512
but it's a readiness signal,
not the evidence of record.

12
00:00:38.512 --> 00:00:41.830
Azure works the same way,
with its own caveats.
